# AWS static site digest

Distilled from Vite's guide, the Amazon CloudFront developer guide (origin
access control, viewer HTTPS, standard logging, response headers), the
Amazon S3 user guide's security best practices and versioning pages, the
AWS WAF developer guide, the CDK developer guide and its assertions module,
and CDK Nag's rule list (see `SOURCES.md`). `[lint: x]` = enforced by
`make check`: an ESLint rule or the compiler; `[nag: x]` = a CDK Nag rule that
fails `cdk synth`; `[test]` = an assertion in `infra/standard.test.ts` or a
stack's own test; `[review]` = check it yourself. `docs/well-architected.md`
says why each rule matters.

## The page

- `index.html` is the entry Vite builds; `src/main.ts` finds the page's
  elements and wires their events to pure functions in `src/`, which never
  touch the DOM. [review]
- The page runs in the browser: no Node global or module in `src/`.
  [lint: no-restricted-globals, no-restricted-imports]
- Write what a visitor typed or a request returned as text
  (`textContent`), never as HTML (`innerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`). [review]
- Visitors see the page, never the console: an error they must know of is
  shown on the page. [lint: no-console]
- Nothing secret in `src/`, `index.html` or `public/`: everything the site
  holds is public. [review]

## The build

- Vite builds the site at synth, through the asset's local bundling
  (`vite build` into the asset's folder); never Docker, which CI and agents
  lack. `npm run build` writes the same into `dist/`, and `npm run dev`
  serves it with hot reload. [review]
- What the page imports from `src/` is bundled and minified under hashed
  names; `public/` is copied as it is, so a file there is referenced by its
  path from the root (`/favicon.svg`). [review]
- A deploy copies the build into the bucket (`BucketDeployment`) beside
  earlier builds' files, so a browser holding an earlier `index.html` still
  finds its hashed assets, and clears CloudFront's cache (`/*`). Nothing
  else writes to the bucket. [review]

## The S3 buckets

- The site's bucket is private: public access blocked, no website hosting,
  read only by the distribution. [nag: AwsSolutions-S2, AwsSolutions-S5]
- Requests without TLS are refused. [nag: AwsSolutions-S10]
- Encrypted at rest with S3-managed keys. [test: encrypts every bucket]
- Its access is logged to the `Logs` bucket (`s3/`). [nag: AwsSolutions-S1]
- In production versioned, kept when the stack goes (`RemovalPolicy.RETAIN`),
  and each earlier version expires after `versionsKeptDays`: 35 days, or
  `retentionDays` when longer. In development unversioned, `DESTROY`, its
  files deleted with the stack. Build it with the tier's `TIER_RULES`.
  [test: the removal policies, the versions of data buckets]
- The `Logs` bucket holds only logs: each expires after the tier's log
  retention, and the bucket, `RETAIN` in both tiers, empties itself.
  CloudFront writes its logs with ACLs, so its objects are the writer's
  (`ObjectOwnership.OBJECT_WRITER`). [review]
- Never change a bucket's logical id: the deploy makes a new, empty bucket
  and keeps the old one outside the stack. [review]

## CloudFront

- The distribution reads the bucket through origin access control
  (`S3BucketOrigin.withOriginAccessControl`). [nag: AwsSolutions-CFR7]
- HTTP is redirected to HTTPS, and every answer carries AWS's managed
  `SECURITY_HEADERS` policy (HSTS, no sniffing, no framing, a strict
  referrer policy). [review]
- With the `DomainName` and `CertificateArn` parameters, the site answers on
  that domain with that ACM certificate (in us-east-1), by SNI, TLS 1.2 or
  later; without them, on CloudFront's own domain. The rule
  `DomainNeedsCertificate` refuses a domain without a certificate.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-CFR4, acknowledged for CloudFront's own domain]
- Standard logs go to the `Logs` bucket (`cloudfront/`).
  [nag: AwsSolutions-CFR3]
- `index.html` is the default root object; HTTP/2 and HTTP/3; the
  cheapest price class (`PRICE_CLASS_100`). [review]
- No geographic restriction: the site is for anyone.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-CFR1, acknowledged]

## The firewall

- In production, an AWS WAF web ACL (scope `CLOUDFRONT`, made in us-east-1)
  in front of the distribution: AWS's managed rule groups for addresses
  with a bad reputation, common attacks and known bad inputs, and a limit of
  2,000 requests in five minutes per address. In development none.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-CFR2, acknowledged in development]
- A rule that blocks visitors the site wants is changed in the stack, with
  the reason, never switched off in the console. [review]

## The copying function

- `BucketDeployment` makes the function that copies the site; it runs only
  on a deploy. Its own role is allowed to write only its log group,
  `DeployLogs`, kept for the tier's log retention, never AWS's managed
  `AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole`. [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM4; test: log groups
  keep logs for a set time]
- Its wildcard permissions, granted by `BucketDeployment`, are acknowledged
  one by one with the reason; never add another. [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM5;
  test: no wildcard action]
- X-Ray traces it in production. [review]
{{- if $operations }}

## Alarms

- The stack's `Alarms` builds every alarm with `cdk-monitoring-constructs`,
  to the stack's one encrypted topic, with a dashboard: the distribution's
  5xx error rate and the copying function's errors, throttles and p99
  duration. A new resource gets its alarms from `alarms.monitoring`.
  [test: the alarms and topic]
- What each alarm means and what to do first is in `docs/runbook.md`;
  a new alarm adds its row there, as a Standard change. [review]
{{- end }}

## The CDK app

- `infra/main.ts`, `infra/standard.ts` and `infra/standard.test.ts` belong
  to the Standard; the
  Framework's stack is in `infra/stacks.ts`, which `defineStacks` returns.
  [review]
- No account in `env`, no `fromLookup`, so synth needs no account. The
  stack's region is us-east-1, because CloudFront takes its firewall and
  certificate only there. [review]
- Every stack carries the Standard's tags (`c4factory:project`,
  `c4factory:system`, `c4factory:container`). [test: tags every stack]
- Every stateful resource says its removal policy. [test]
- A CDK Nag finding is fixed in the resource. An acknowledgement
  (`Validations.of(construct).acknowledge({ id, reason })`), on the construct
  the finding's path names or an ancestor of it, names one finding
  on one construct, with a reason specific to this system. [review]
- `cdk.json`'s feature flags are the ones `cdk init` recommends for the
  pinned CDK; a task never turns one off. [review]

## Tests

- No test calls AWS, opens a socket or needs credentials. [review]
- The page's logic is tested with plain vitest in Node, through the pure
  modules `src/main.ts` wires; a test that needs the DOM brings its own
  environment. [review]
- The stack is tested with `Template.fromStack` from
  `aws-cdk-lib/assertions`: `hasResourceProperties`, `resourceCountIs`,
  `Match`. Assert what the issue needs (a header, a rule, a setting), not
  the whole template. Synthesizing it builds the site with Vite. [review]
