{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}## The server

- `src/main.ts` is the entry point the instances run (`node server.mjs`
  under systemd): it reads the environment, creates the SDK clients and the
  store, and starts the server from `src/server.ts`. [review]
- Create SDK clients and read configuration once, at start-up; every
  request reuses them and their connections. Never create a client per
  request. [review]
- Read `process.env` only in `src/main.ts`, once, and fail at start-up with a
  message naming the variable when one is missing: systemd restarts the
  server, and the load balancer's health check keeps traffic off it. [review]
- `src/server.ts` adapts HTTP to `answer` and nothing else: it reads the body
  up to a limit (413 past it), takes the request id from the load balancer's
  `X-Amzn-Trace-Id`, and writes one log entry per request. The work is in
  functions that take what they need (a store, a log), so tests call them
  without a server. [review]
- `GET /health` answers 200 without touching DynamoDB: it says the process
  serves, and a DynamoDB outage must not take every instance out of the
  target group at once. [review]
- The server's keep-alive outlasts the load balancer's 60-second idle
  timeout (`keepAliveTimeout` 65 s, `headersTimeout` above it), or a request
  racing a close gets a 502. [test]
- On `SIGTERM` (an instance being replaced or scaled in) stop accepting
  connections, finish the requests in flight, then let the process end.
  Never `process.exit()`. [lint: no-restricted-properties]
- Await every promise, or void it on purpose with a comment: a rejected
  promise nobody awaits ends the process.
  [lint: @typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises]
- Don't keep request data in module-scope variables: the next request on the
  same instance would see it, and other instances would not. [review]

{{ else }}## The handler

{{ if $go -}}
- `src/main.go`'s `main` builds what every invocation shares, then calls
  `lambda.Start` with a function that takes the context and an
  `*events.APIGatewayV2HTTPRequest` and returns an
  `events.APIGatewayV2HTTPResponse`: a status code, headers and a string
  body. API Gateway's HTTP API sends payload format 2.0. [review]
- Load the SDK's configuration (`config.LoadDefaultConfig`) and create
  clients in `main`, once per execution environment; warm invocations reuse
  them and their connections. Never create a client inside the handler.
  [review]
- Read the environment only in `main`, once, and fail at start-up with a
  message naming the variable when one is missing: log it and `os.Exit(1)`,
  the one place an exit is right. [review]
- Keep `main` to wiring; the work is in functions that take what they need
  (a context, a store, a logger), so tests call them without Lambda. [review]
- Pass the invocation's `ctx` to every SDK call: it carries the function's
  deadline, so a slow call is cancelled before Lambda kills the environment.
  [review]
- No goroutine outlives the handler: work left running after it returns is
  frozen with the environment and may never finish. [review]
- Never `panic`, `log.Fatal` or `os.Exit` once `lambda.Start` runs: return
  an error response instead. [review]
- Don't keep request data in package-level variables: the next invocation in
  the same environment would see it. [review]
{{- else if $py -}}
- `app/handler.py` defines `handler(event, context)`, which takes an HTTP
  API event (payload format 2.0, a dict) and returns a dict: a status code,
  headers and a string body. [review]
- Create boto3 clients and resources and read configuration at module scope,
  once per execution environment; warm invocations reuse them and their
  connections. Never create one inside the handler. [review]
- Read `os.environ` only in `app/handler.py`, once, and fail at start-up
  with a message naming the variable when one is missing. [review]
- Keep the handler to wiring; the work is in functions that take what they
